# emptty user session, symlinked to ~/.config/emptty by install.sh.
#
# Two jobs, and they pull against each other.
#
# 1. Offer a session picker, so sway and Hyprland can both be logged into.
# 2. Keep LoginShell, because emptty starts the session directly from its own
#    process. Without a login shell the session inherits emptty's bare PATH
#    (/usr/bin:/usr/sbin) and never reads ~/.bash_profile, so everything
#    launched from the compositor loses ~/.local/bin and ~/.cargo/bin. That
#    shows up as GUI apps failing to find tools that work fine in a terminal.
#
# .desktop files under the session paths have no LoginShell key, so a plain
# picker would drop it. Selection=true is what reconciles the two: emptty then
# treats THIS file as a wrapper (its own .xinitrc, in emptty's words), skips
# its Exec, and runs
#
#     <LoginShell> ~/.config/emptty <Exec of the chosen session>
#
# so the login shell wraps whichever session was picked. The key=value lines
# below are read by emptty as config and are also valid bash, which is why the
# quotes on LoginShell matter: emptty strips them, bash needs them so that
# "--login" is not run as a command.
#
# Sessions offered:
#   Sway      /usr/share/wayland-sessions/sway.desktop   (stock, packaged)
#   Hyprland  ~/.config/emptty-custom-sessions/hyprland.desktop
#
# The stock sway.desktop is fine to use directly now. It could not be used
# before because it had no way to carry LoginShell; the wrapper supplies it.
# Its Exec is a bare "sway", which the login PATH resolves to /usr/bin/sway --
# the locally patched sway 1.11 carrying lock_visible_namespace, not the
# packaged binary despite the path. Do not let a package install overwrite it.
#
# Recovery: if a session ever fails to start, switch to another TTY and
# `rm ~/.config/emptty` to fall back to the stock sway.desktop entry.

Selection="true"
LoginShell="/bin/bash --login"

# emptty only starts dbus-launch for sessions it execs directly, and a wrapped
# session is not one of those, so the session bus is ours to provide. The bus
# lives exactly as long as the compositor, which is what dbus-launch's
# --exit-with-session gives and what emptty did before.
exec dbus-run-session -- "$@"
